#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1186
msgid "The Git commands:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1195
msgid "`.gitignore`: what Git must not keep"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1197
msgid "A text file `.gitignore` in the project folder lists the files and folders Git ignores. For a data project, this is not optional:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1220
msgid "Data and passwords never go into Git"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1221
msgid "Git keeps **everything** forever: a file that was committed once stays in the history, even after you delete it. Real data of the bank, exports with client names, files with passwords must never be committed - not even \"just for a moment\". Put them in `.gitignore` **before** the first `git add`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1242
msgid "The patterns of `.gitignore` are the same kind as `glob` ([lecture 8](./08-errors-and-files.md#find-files-glob)). The module `fnmatch` tests a name against them - which files of a project would Git ignore?"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1257
msgid "Good commits"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1259
msgid "**Small**: one change, one commit - \"Add the reject file to the loader\", not \"work of Tuesday\"."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1261
msgid "**A message that says what and why**: in the imperative, like a title: \"Fix the date format of the payments file\"."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1263
msgid "**Working code**: commit when the script runs."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1265
msgid "Git in VS Code"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1267
msgid "The **Source Control** view (`Ctrl+Shift+G`) does the same with buttons: the changed files, **+** to stage, a message box and **Commit**, the history, and the changes of a file side by side. Use whatever you prefer - the commands are the same underneath."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1272
msgid "In your Homework repository: `git status`, then `git log --oneline -5`. Change one line in a task file, and look at `git diff`. Then `git restore <file>` to undo it."
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1276
msgid "7. Branches and merge"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1278
msgid "A **branch** is a separate line of commits. The main line is usually called `main`. Work on a change in its own branch; when it is ready, **merge** it into `main`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1296
msgid "Why branches, for a report script: `main` always holds the version that produced the last official numbers; the changes for next month are made on a branch, and merged after they are checked."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1300
msgid "A conflict"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1302
msgid "When two branches change the **same lines** of a file, Git cannot decide alone. `git merge` stops and marks the place in the file:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1313
msgid "Edit the file to the version you want (delete the markers), then:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1320
msgid "VS Code shows conflicts with buttons: **Accept Current**, **Accept Incoming**, **Accept Both**."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1324
msgid "In a test folder: `git init`, commit a file `rules.py` with `LGD = 0.45`; create a branch, change it to `0.40`, commit; back on `main`, change it to `0.50`, commit; merge the branch. Resolve the conflict."
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1329
msgid "8. GitHub: remotes and pull requests"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1331
msgid "A remote repository"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1333
msgid "A **remote** is a copy of the repository on a server. The Homework repository of this course has two (how-to page): `upstream` (the course) and `origin` (yours)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1343
msgid "The usual day: `git pull` before you start, commit as you work, `git push` at the end."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1345
msgid "A pull request: a review before the merge"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1347
msgid "On GitHub (and GitLab, Azure DevOps, Bitbucket), a **pull request** (a *merge request* in GitLab) asks: \"please merge my branch into `main`\". A colleague reads the changes line by line, comments, and approves - the four-eyes principle, for code:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1360
msgid "Without GitHub"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1362
msgid "If your company does not allow github.com, everything of sections 6 and 7 works on your computer alone, and a company server (GitLab, Azure DevOps, Bitbucket) works like section 8 - only the address in `git clone` is different."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1367
msgid "On GitHub, open your own Homework repository: find the list of commits, open one, and look at its changes. Compare with `git log -p -1` in the terminal."
msgstr ""

