#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1504
msgid "In a test repository, commit a file three times with different `LGD` values. Use `git log -S \"LGD = 0.40\" --oneline` to find the commit that introduced `0.40`, and `git revert` it."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/09-modules-and-git.md:1509
msgid "`git log -S \"LGD = 0.40\" --oneline` lists the commits that added or removed that text; take the hash of the one that added it, and `git revert <hash>`. Git opens an editor for the message of the new commit (save and close it); the file is back to the value before that commit - unless a later commit changed the same line, which gives a conflict to resolve."
